  "contracts": {
    "thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On a compact interval $K\\subset J$, let $Z_N$, $D_N$, and $Q_N$ be the first $N$ partial sums of $\\sum_i g_i e^{-\\beta E_i}$, $-\\sum_i g_iE_ie^{-\\beta E_i}$, and $\\sum_i g_iE_i^2e^{-\\beta E_i}$. Since $|E_i|^r\\le1+|E_i|^2$ for $r=0,1,2$, each tail is bounded uniformly on $K$ by the corresponding tail of $\\sum_i a_i$, which tends to zero [A1]. Thus all three sequences converge uniformly; call their limits $Z,D,Q$. Each finite partial sum has the displayed derivatives by the chain rule [F5] and the exponential derivative [F2]. Each partial sum is continuous, so the uniform limits are continuous by the $\\varepsilon/3$ definition of continuity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For an interior $\\beta$ and sufficiently small $h\\ne0$, apply [F4] to $Z_N$ on the interval with endpoints $\\beta$ and $\\beta+h$: its difference quotient is $D_N(\\xi_{N,h})$ for some intermediate $\\xi_{N,h}$. Choose $N=N(h)$ so that $\\sup_K|Z_N-Z|<h^2$ and $\\sup_K|D_N-D|<|h|$. The difference quotient for $Z$ then differs from $D(\\beta)$ by at most $2|h|+|h|+\\sup_{|t-\\beta|\\le|h|}|D(t)-D(\\beta)|$, which tends to zero by continuity of $D$. Hence $Z'=D$. Applying the same argument to $D_N$ and $Q_N$ proves $D'=Q$, so the displayed derivatives hold.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The summands of $Z$ are positive and at least one is present, hence $0<Z<\\infty$ on $K$; summing the definition of $p_i$ gives $\\sum_i p_i=1$. The envelope also gives $\\sum_i p_i|E_i|$ and $\\sum_i p_iE_i^2$ finite. Differentiating $\\log Z$ using [F3] yields $-Z'/Z=\\bar E$ and $Z''/Z-(Z'/Z)^2=\\langle E^2\\rangle-\\bar E^2=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Since $p_i/g_i=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$, its logarithm is $-\\beta E_i-\\log Z$. The finite first moment makes $\\sum_i p_i|\\log(p_i/g_i)|\\le |\\beta|\\sum_i p_i|E_i|+|\\log Z|\\sum_i p_i<\\infty$; summing the identity gives $H_g=\\beta\\bar E+\\log Z$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The nonempty state-set hypothesis makes Z positive; step 3.1 uses it explicitly."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–3.1 allow zero energies and zero observables; the partition sum remains positive because the state set is nonempty."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $I,E_i,g_i,J,Z$ be as in [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]]. Boundary one: The claim is not a one-element counting or normalization assertion.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $I,E_i,g_i,J,Z$ be as in [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]]. Suppose that for every compact interval $K\\subset J$ there is a summable sequence $a_i\\ge0$ such that $g_i(1+|E_i|^2)\\exp(-\\beta E_i)\\le a_i$ for every $i\\in I$ and $\\beta\\in K$. Then $Z$ is positive, finite, and $C^2$ on $J$, with $Z'(\\beta)=-\\sum_i g_iE_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ and $Z''(\\beta)=\\sum_i g_iE_i^2\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$. The probabilities $p_i$ are normalized; their mean $\\bar E$ and variance are finite and satisfy $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E$ and $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$. Moreover $H_g(p)=\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E$, and its defining series is absolutely convergent.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "05887254b08ce3ba6a57d62ddd4604ace4e6f54623c5dc47096313dffdc859a2",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
