    "thm-tdmath-dominated-convergence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-fatou",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to[0,\\infty]$ be measurable. Then $\\int\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} f_n\\,d\\mu\\le\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\int f_n\\,d\\mu$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-monotone-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to[0,\\infty]$ be measurable with $f_n\\le f_{n+1}$ and $f_n\\to f$ pointwise. Then $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu$, including infinite values. Also, for measurable $f,g\\ge0$, $\\int(f+g)\\,d\\mu=\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\int g\\,d\\mu$. For every $c\\ge0$, $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$; the integral is also linear on real integrable functions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ be a measurable space and let\n$f,g : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ be measurable. Then:\n\n1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;\n2. $\\max(f,g)$, $\\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;\n3. if $f+g$ is pointwise defined, then $f+g$ is measurable;\n4. with the convention of\n   [[rem-zero-times-infinity-convention-for-pointwise-products]], the pointwise\n   product $fg$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$, $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$, and $(Z,\\mathcal{C})$ be measurable\nspaces. If $f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Y,\\mathcal{B})$ is measurable and\n$g : (Y,\\mathcal{B}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable, then\n$g \\circ f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable.\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is measurable and $g$ is a Borel measurable function on\nits codomain, then $g \\circ f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sequential-suprema-infima-limsup-liminf-and-pointwise-limits-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ be a measurable space and let\n$f_n : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ be measurable for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\nThen the functions\n\n$$\\sup_n f_n,\\qquad \\inf_n f_n,\\qquad \\limsup_n f_n,\\qquad \\liminf_n f_n$$\n\nare measurable. The set\n\n$$\\{\\, x : \\lim_n f_n(x) \\text{ exists in } \\overline{\\mathbb{R}} \\,\\}$$\n\nis measurable. In particular, if $f_n \\to f$ pointwise, then $f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-complex-plane-is-complete",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The metric space $\\mathbb C$ is complete. A sequence $z_n=x_n+iy_n$ converges to $x+iy$ exactly when $x_n\\to x$ and $y_n\\to y$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]], [[thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "The set on which $(f_n)$ is Cauchy is measurable: write it as the countable intersection/union/intersection of sets $\\{|f_n-f_k|<1/m\\}$ using [F4]. On this set the finite real and imaginary limits exist; their measurable limit functions, set to zero on its complement, define the measurable $f_0$. The assumed almost-everywhere convergence gives $f_0=f$ outside a measurable null set. Enlarge that set by the countably many measurable domination exceptions to a measurable null set $N$. Set $f_n$ and $f_0$ to zero on $N$, which preserves their measurability. A nonnegative measurable function supported on $N$ has integral zero by the simple-minorant definition, so these changes preserve every defined integral. Convergence and domination now hold everywhere, and $|f_0|\\le g$ proves integrability. This does not redefine the potentially nonmeasurable $f$ and then claim it was measurable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "First take all functions real. The nonnegative functions $f_n+g$ converge pointwise to $f_0+g$. Fatou gives $\\int(f_0+g)\\le\\liminf_n\\int(f_n+g)=\\int g+\\liminf_n\\int f_n$, so $\\int f_0\\le\\liminf_n\\int f_n$. Applying Fatou to $g-f_n\\ge0$ gives $\\int(g-f_0)\\le\\liminf_n\\int(g-f_n)=\\int g-\\limsup_n\\int f_n$, hence $\\limsup_n\\int f_n\\le\\int f_0$. Additivity of integrable signed integrals follows by writing each as a difference of its positive and negative parts and using [F3]. The inequalities imply convergence of integrals.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "For complex $f_n$, apply step 2.1 separately to their real and imaginary parts, each dominated by $g$; their integral limits combine to $\\int f_0$. To prove $L^1$ convergence, apply Fatou to the nonnegative functions $2g-|f_n-f_0|$, which converge to $2g$; it gives $2\\int g\\le2\\int g-\\limsup_n\\int|f_n-f_0|$, so the latter limsup is zero. Therefore $\\int|f_n-f_0|\\to0$ as claimed.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable with $f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere. Suppose $g\\ge0$ is measurable and $\\int g\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for every $n$. Let $f_0(x)$ be the finite complex pointwise limit of $(f_n(x))$ where it exists, and $0$ elsewhere. Then $f_0$ is measurable, equals $f$ almost everywhere, is integrable, and $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\to\\int f_0\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int|f_n-f_0|\\,d\\mu\\to0$. If the specified $f$ is measurable, these same conclusions hold with $f$ in place of $f_0$. In particular this is automatic when $\\mu$ is complete. On a noncomplete measure space an arbitrary almost-everywhere representative need not be measurable, so no integral of such a nonmeasurable $f$ is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f7d0a48c760253ccbb765dd19a3aae267a95c68b33d4cd7e3475031af7655f5f",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "evidence": "The measure space may have empty X. The canonical measurable limit f_0 and every integral are then the empty function/zero integral, consistent with the Statement and proof1.1–3.1; no integral of a nonmeasurable representative is introduced."
        },
