          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable with $f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere. Suppose $g\\ge0$ is measurable and $\\int g\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for every $n$. Let $f_0(x)$ be the finite complex pointwise limit of $(f_n(x))$ where it exists, and $0$ elsewhere. Then $f_0$ is measurable, equals $f$ almost everywhere, is integrable, and $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\to\\int f_0\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int|f_n-f_0|\\,d\\mu\\to0$. If the specified $f$ is measurable, these same conclusions hold with $f$ in place of $f_0$. In particular this is automatic when $\\mu$ is complete. On a noncomplete measure space an arbitrary almost-everywhere representative need not be measurable, so no integral of such a nonmeasurable $f$ is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f7d0a48c760253ccbb765dd19a3aae267a95c68b33d4cd7e3475031af7655f5f",
