        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-monotone-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-monotone-convergence.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to[0,\\infty]$ be measurable with $f_n\\le f_{n+1}$ and $f_n\\to f$ pointwise. Then $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu$, including infinite values. Also, for measurable $f,g\\ge0$, $\\int(f+g)\\,d\\mu=\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\int g\\,d\\mu$. For every $c\\ge0$, $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$; the integral is also linear on real integrable functions.",
