    "thm-tdmath-measurable-canonical-identities": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable with $f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere. Suppose $g\\ge0$ is measurable and $\\int g\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for every $n$. Let $f_0(x)$ be the finite complex pointwise limit of $(f_n(x))$ where it exists, and $0$ elsewhere. Then $f_0$ is measurable, equals $f$ almost everywhere, is integrable, and $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\to\\int f_0\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int|f_n-f_0|\\,d\\mu\\to0$. If the specified $f$ is measurable, these same conclusions hold with $f$ in place of $f_0$. In particular this is automatic when $\\mu$ is complete. On a noncomplete measure space an arbitrary almost-everywhere representative need not be measurable, so no integral of such a nonmeasurable $f$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ be a measurable space and let\n$f,g : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ be measurable. Then:\n\n1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;\n2. $\\max(f,g)$, $\\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;\n3. if $f+g$ is pointwise defined, then $f+g$ is measurable;\n4. with the convention of\n   [[rem-zero-times-infinity-convention-for-pointwise-products]], the pointwise\n   product $fg$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$, $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$, and $(Z,\\mathcal{C})$ be measurable\nspaces. If $f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Y,\\mathcal{B})$ is measurable and\n$g : (Y,\\mathcal{B}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable, then\n$g \\circ f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable.\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is measurable and $g$ is a Borel measurable function on\nits codomain, then $g \\circ f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each fixed parameter, measurable energy/observable coordinates and [F7] make every finite linear combination, exponential weight, weighted first/second moment and parameter difference quotient measurable; their displayed pointwise derivative limits are the same measurable compositions. For $\\beta$ in the interior of a compact interval $K\\subset J$, the difference quotient of $e^{-\\beta E(x)}$ tends pointwise to $-E(x)e^{-\\beta E(x)}$. The mean-value theorem [F5] bounds its absolute value, for small $h$, by $|E|e^{-\\theta E}$ for an intermediate $\\theta\\in K$; this is at most $G_K$ because $|E|\\le1+|E|^2$. Dominated convergence [F2] therefore gives $Z'=-\\int E e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$. Applying the same argument to $-E e^{-\\beta E}$, dominated by $G_K$, gives $Z''=\\int E^2e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$; dominated convergence also gives continuity of both derivatives.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F5",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "The density $p_\\beta$ is nonnegative and integrates to one. The envelope makes its first and second energy moments finite. Differentiating $\\log Z$ using [F4] gives $-Z'/Z=\\int E p_\\beta d\\mu$ and $Z''/Z-(Z'/Z)^2=\\int E^2p_\\beta d\\mu-(\\int Ep_\\beta d\\mu)^2$. The latter is the variance by expanding its defining square.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\log p_\\beta=-\\beta E-\\log Z$ almost everywhere and $\\int |E|p_\\beta d\\mu<\\infty$, the entropy integrand is absolutely integrable. Substitution and normalization yield $-\\int p_\\beta\\log p_\\beta d\\mu=\\beta\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta+\\log Z$. This entropy depends on the chosen reference measure $\\mu$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "empty: The claim has no empty-set or empty-domain assertion; its stated object domain is nonempty or the boundary is not separate.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $E:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable, and let $J\\subset\\mathbb R$ be open. Suppose $Z(\\beta):=\\int e^{-\\beta E}\\,d\\mu$ satisfies $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ on $J$, and for every compact interval $K\\subset J$ there is $G_K\\in L^1(\\mu)$ with $(1+|E|^2)e^{-\\beta E}\\le G_K$ pointwise for every $\\beta\\in K$. Define $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z(\\beta)$, $\\langle Y\\rangle_\\beta:=\\int Yp_\\beta\\,d\\mu$ for integrable $Y$, and $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E):=\\int(E-\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta)^2p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$. Then $Z\\in C^2(J)$, $Z'=-\\int E e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$, $Z''=\\int E^2e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$, and $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta$, $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$. The reference-relative entropy $-\\int p_\\beta\\log p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$ is finite and equals $\\log Z+\\beta\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "1b2fb46cc08554a9168bf0a518b854823b13ccaf52cb1a8891f87b767eaecba3",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
