    "thm-tdmath-independent-bulk-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite probability space** is a pair $(\\Omega,w)$ consisting of a finite set $\\Omega$ and a function $w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $w(\\omega)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$ and\n$$\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)=1.$$\nThe elements of $\\Omega$ are **outcomes**. Every subset $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ is an **event**, and its probability is\n$$\\mathbb P(A):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega).$$\n\nAn outcome of weight zero remains an outcome. Thus an event can be nonempty and still have probability zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-product-of-finite-probability-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $((\\Omega_i,w_i))_{i\\in I}$ be a finite indexed family of finite probability spaces. Their **product probability space** has outcome set\n$$\\Omega:=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\Omega_i$$\nand assigns $\\omega=(\\omega_i)_{i\\in I}$ the weight\n$$w(\\omega):=\\prod_{i\\in I}w_i(\\omega_i).$$\nFor $I=\\varnothing$, the product outcome set is the singleton containing the empty function and its weight is the empty product $1$. Normalization and independence of coordinate events are proved in [[thm-product-probability-has-independent-coordinate-events]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-product-probability-has-independent-coordinate-events",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The weights in every finite product of finite probability spaces are nonnegative and sum to $1$. For events $A_i\\subseteq\\Omega_i$, the coordinate events\n$$C_i:=\\{\\omega\\in\\prod_{j\\in I}\\Omega_j:\\omega_i\\in A_i\\}$$\nare mutually independent, and for every $J\\subseteq I$,\n$$\\mathbb P\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{j\\in J}C_j\\right)=\\prod_{j\\in J}\\mathbb P_j(A_j).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-expectation-of-products-of-independent-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is a finite mutually independent family of real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\prod_{i\\in I}X_i\\right]=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\mathbb E[X_i].$$\nFor $I=\\varnothing$, both sides equal $1$. The converse is not asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a finite family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}X_i\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{Var}(X_i)+2\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\subseteq I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).$$\nEquivalently, it is $\\sum_{i,j\\in I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$. The empty sum has variance zero, and the singleton formula is the identity.\nIn the first display, the second sum is over two-element subsets of $I$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-finite-sum-reindexing-and-fubini",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,+,0)$ be a commutative monoid and let all index sets below be finite.\n\n1. If $h:T\\to S$ is a bijection and $a:S\\to M$, then $\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{h(t)}=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s$.\n2. If $S$ and $T$ are disjoint and $a:S\\cup T\\to M$, then $\\sum_{u\\in S\\cup T}a_u=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s+\\sum_{t\\in T}a_t$.\n3. If $a:S\\times T\\to M$, then\n   $$\\sum_{(s,t)\\in S\\times T}a_{s,t}=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\left(\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{s,t}\\right)=\\sum_{t\\in T}\\left(\\sum_{s\\in S}a_{s,t}\\right).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By finite Fubini, $Z_N=\\sum_{(i_1,\\ldots,i_N)}\\prod_{k=1}^Ne^{-\\beta E_{i_k}}=\\prod_{k=1}^N\\sum_{i_k\\in\\Omega_0}e^{-\\beta E_{i_k}}=Z_1^N$. This is an exact finite identity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Under the product law, the coordinate energies $E(i_k)$ are mutually independent and each has mean $\\bar E$ and variance $v:=\\operatorname{Var}_q(E)$. Linearity gives $\\mathbb E H_N=N\\bar E$; the covariance terms between distinct coordinates vanish by independence, so [F3] gives $\\operatorname{Var}(H_N)=Nv$. Dividing by $N$ and $N^2$ proves the mean and variance formulas.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the event $|H_N/N-\\bar E|\\ge\\varepsilon$, the nonnegative random variable $(H_N/N-\\bar E)^2$ is at least $\\varepsilon^2$. Summing over that event gives $\\varepsilon^2\\mathbb P(|H_N/N-\\bar E|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\mathbb E[(H_N/N-\\bar E)^2]=v/N$, proving the probability bound without any independence assertion beyond the product model.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $\\beta\\ne0$, substitute $Z_N=Z_1^N$ and apply the logarithm product law: $-\\beta^{-1}N^{-1}\\log Z_N=-\\beta^{-1}N^{-1}N\\log Z_1=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_1$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement Omega_0 is nonempty and N>=1. This gives positive Z_1 and excludes the N=0 energy-per-particle denominator, while zero single-site variance is permitted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\Omega_0$ be a nonempty finite set, let $E:\\Omega_0\\to\\mathbb R$, and fix $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$. Define $Z_1=\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega_0}e^{-\\beta E_i}$ and the canonical law $q_i=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z_1$, and define $\\bar E:=\\sum_iq_iE_i$. For $N\\ge1$, on $\\Omega_0^N$ define $H_N(i_1,\\ldots,i_N)=\\sum_{k=1}^NE_{i_k}$ with the product law. Then $Z_N=Z_1^N$, $\\mathbb E[H_N/N]=\\bar E$, and $\\operatorname{Var}(H_N/N)=\\operatorname{Var}_q(E)/N$. Consequently, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, $\\mathbb P(|H_N/N-\\bar E|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\operatorname{Var}_q(E)/(N\\varepsilon^2)$. If $\\beta\\ne0$, the mathematical free-energy density $-\\beta^{-1}N^{-1}\\log Z_N$ is exactly $-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_1$ for every $N$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "33535aeeb6ed3a7e7ffd74dfe7cd52a07e65060ee89efba00f96f278680a894a",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
