      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
          "carrier": "items/def-finite-probability-space-and-event.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **finite probability space** is a pair $(\\Omega,w)$ consisting of a finite set $\\Omega$ and a function $w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $w(\\omega)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$ and\n$$\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)=1.$$\nThe elements of $\\Omega$ are **outcomes**. Every subset $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ is an **event**, and its probability is\n$$\\mathbb P(A):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega).$$\n\nAn outcome of weight zero remains an outcome. Thus an event can be nonempty and still have probability zero.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-product-of-finite-probability-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/def-product-of-finite-probability-spaces.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $((\\Omega_i,w_i))_{i\\in I}$ be a finite indexed family of finite probability spaces. Their **product probability space** has outcome set\n$$\\Omega:=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\Omega_i$$\nand assigns $\\omega=(\\omega_i)_{i\\in I}$ the weight\n$$w(\\omega):=\\prod_{i\\in I}w_i(\\omega_i).$$\nFor $I=\\varnothing$, the product outcome set is the singleton containing the empty function and its weight is the empty product $1$. Normalization and independence of coordinate events are proved in [[thm-product-probability-has-independent-coordinate-events]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-product-probability-has-independent-coordinate-events",
          "carrier": "items/thm-product-probability-has-independent-coordinate-events.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The weights in every finite product of finite probability spaces are nonnegative and sum to $1$. For events $A_i\\subseteq\\Omega_i$, the coordinate events\n$$C_i:=\\{\\omega\\in\\prod_{j\\in I}\\Omega_j:\\omega_i\\in A_i\\}$$\nare mutually independent, and for every $J\\subseteq I$,\n$$\\mathbb P\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{j\\in J}C_j\\right)=\\prod_{j\\in J}\\mathbb P_j(A_j).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "carrier": "items/def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-expectation-of-products-of-independent-random-variables",
          "carrier": "items/thm-expectation-of-products-of-independent-random-variables.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "If $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is a finite mutually independent family of real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\prod_{i\\in I}X_i\\right]=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\mathbb E[X_i].$$\nFor $I=\\varnothing$, both sides equal $1$. The converse is not asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "carrier": "items/def-variance-and-covariance.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum",
          "carrier": "items/thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For a finite family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}X_i\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{Var}(X_i)+2\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\subseteq I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).$$\nEquivalently, it is $\\sum_{i,j\\in I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$. The empty sum has variance zero, and the singleton formula is the identity.\nIn the first display, the second sum is over two-element subsets of $I$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "carrier": "items/def-real-exponential-function-and-e.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "carrier": "items/def-natural-logarithm.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "carrier": "items/thm-natural-logarithm-laws.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-finite-sum-reindexing-and-fubini",
          "carrier": "items/lem-finite-sum-reindexing-and-fubini.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(M,+,0)$ be a commutative monoid and let all index sets below be finite.\n\n1. If $h:T\\to S$ is a bijection and $a:S\\to M$, then $\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{h(t)}=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s$.\n2. If $S$ and $T$ are disjoint and $a:S\\cup T\\to M$, then $\\sum_{u\\in S\\cup T}a_u=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s+\\sum_{t\\in T}a_t$.\n3. If $a:S\\times T\\to M$, then\n   $$\\sum_{(s,t)\\in S\\times T}a_{s,t}=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\left(\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{s,t}\\right)=\\sum_{t\\in T}\\left(\\sum_{s\\in S}a_{s,t}\\right).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F5.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 1.1, 3.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-tdmath-ising-chain-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout this page $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), in particular an ordered field\n([[def-ordered-field]]) and a field ([[def-field]]), and $\\mathbb{N}$ is the set\nof natural numbers ([[def-natural-numbers]]) with successor $\\sigma(n) = n + 1$\n([[def-nat-addition]]).\n\nLet $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of reals, written $a_k$ for\n$a(k)$. **Finite sums and finite products of $a$ are defined by recursion on the\nupper index**, which is legitimate because of the recursion theorem\n([[thm-recursion]]). That theorem produces a function of one variable, so the\nrunning index has to be carried along inside the value: applying it to the set\n$A = \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$, the starting element $(0, 0)$ and the function\n$f(n, s) = (\\sigma(n),\\, s + a_n)$ gives a unique\n$g : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$ with\n\n$$g(0) = (0, 0), \\qquad g(\\sigma(n)) = f(g(n)) \\quad (n \\in \\mathbb{N}).$$\n\nWrite $g(n) = \\big(\\pi_1(g(n)),\\, \\Sigma_n\\big)$ for its two coordinates.\n\n**The first coordinate is the index itself, and that is a small induction, not an\nobservation** ([[thm-induction-principle]]). Indeed $\\pi_1(g(0)) = 0$; and if\n$\\pi_1(g(n)) = n$, then\n$g(\\sigma(n)) = f\\big(\\pi_1(g(n)), \\Sigma_n\\big) = \\big(\\sigma(\\pi_1(g(n))),\\, \\Sigma_n + a_{\\pi_1(g(n))}\\big) = \\big(\\sigma(n),\\, \\Sigma_n + a_n\\big)$,\nso $\\pi_1(g(\\sigma(n))) = \\sigma(n)$. By induction $\\pi_1(g(n)) = n$ for every\n$n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Only now may the second coordinate of the two displayed\nclauses be read off, and doing so gives\n\n$$\\Sigma_0 = 0, \\qquad \\Sigma_{\\sigma(n)} = \\Sigma_n + a_n .$$\n\n$\\Sigma$ is moreover the **unique** function $\\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with those\ntwo properties: if $\\Sigma'$ also has them then $n \\mapsto (n, \\Sigma'_n)$ satisfies\nthe two clauses defining $g$, hence equals $g$ by the uniqueness clause of\n[[thm-recursion]], so $\\Sigma' = \\Sigma$.\n\nWe write $\\sum_{k < n} a_k := \\Sigma_n$. The same construction with starting\nelement $(0, 1)$ and $f(n, p) = (\\sigma(n),\\, p \\cdot a_n)$, with the same\ninduction on the first coordinate and the same uniqueness argument, gives the\nunique $\\Pi : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n\n$$\\Pi_0 = 1, \\qquad \\Pi_{\\sigma(n)} = \\Pi_n \\cdot a_n ,$$\n\nand we write $\\prod_{k < n} a_k := \\Pi_n$.\n\n**Notation.** For $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ we abbreviate\n\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k := \\sum_{k < n+1} a_k, \\qquad \\prod_{k=0}^{n} a_k := \\prod_{k < n+1} a_k,$$\n\nand, for a general lower index $m$ with $m \\le n + 1$, writing $d = n + 1 - m$\nfor the number of terms,\n\n$$\\sum_{k=m}^{n} a_k := \\sum_{j < d} a_{m+j}, \\qquad \\prod_{k=m}^{n} a_k := \\prod_{j < d} a_{m+j} .$$\n\nWhen $m = n + 1$ we have $d = 0$ and the sum is **empty**, with value $0$, while\nthe empty product has value $1$. In the same spirit $\\sum_{k=0}^{-1} a_k$ is\nnotation for the empty sum $\\Sigma_0 = 0$ and $\\prod_{k=0}^{-1} a_k$ for the\nempty product $\\Pi_0 = 1$; the index $-1$ never occurs as an element of\n$\\mathbb{N}$ and is only a way of writing \"no terms\".\n\nOnly finitely many values of $a$ enter $\\sum_{k<n} a_k$, so the notation\n$\\sum_{k<n} a_k$ and $\\prod_{k<n} a_k$ is also used for a list\n$a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1}$ of reals given without reference to any extension of the\nlist to all of $\\mathbb{N}$: extend the list by $a_k = 0$ (respectively\n$a_k = 1$) for $k \\ge n$ and apply the definition above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-finite-sum-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b, c : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be sequences of reals, let\n$\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and let $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, with finite sums and\nfinite products as in [[def-finite-sum]]. Then:\n\n1. **Additivity.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n}(a_k + b_k) = \\sum_{k<n} a_k + \\sum_{k<n} b_k$.\n2. **Scaling.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda a_k = \\lambda \\sum_{k<n} a_k$; in particular $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda = n\\lambda$, where $n$ denotes the canonical field image $n\\cdot1_{\\mathbb{R}}$ of the natural n ([[lem-of-q-embeds]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).\n3. **Splitting.** If $m \\le n$ then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} a_k = \\sum_{k<m} a_k + \\sum_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k$, and $\\displaystyle\\prod_{k<n} a_k = \\Big(\\prod_{k<m} a_k\\Big)\\Big(\\prod_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k\\Big)$.\n4. **Monotonicity.** If $a_k \\le b_k$ for all $k < n$ then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} a_k \\le \\sum_{k<n} b_k$. In particular, if $a_k \\ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\sum_{k<n} a_k \\ge 0$, every single term satisfies $a_j \\le \\sum_{k<n} a_k$ for $j < n$, and $\\sum_{k<n} a_k = 0$ forces $a_k = 0$ for every $k < n$.\n5. **Telescoping.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n}\\big(c_{k+1} - c_k\\big) = c_n - c_0$.\n6. **Products.** $\\displaystyle\\prod_{k<n}(a_k b_k) = \\Big(\\prod_{k<n} a_k\\Big)\\Big(\\prod_{k<n} b_k\\Big)$; if $a_k \\ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\prod_{k<n} a_k \\ge 0$, and if $a_k > 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\prod_{k<n} a_k > 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nth-roots-exist",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb{R}$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nFor every $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a \\ge 0$ and every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with\n$n \\ge 1$ there is a **unique** $s \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^n = a$\n([[def-integer-power]]); we write\n\n$$s = a^{1/n} = \\sqrt[n]{a}.$$\n\nMoreover $a^{1/n} > 0$ when $a > 0$, and $a^{1/1} = a$.\n\n**This generalises the published [[thm-of-square-roots]], and the case $n = 2$ is\nnot new.** That theorem already produces the unique $\\sqrt{a} \\ge 0$ with\n$(\\sqrt{a})^2 = a$, and it is cited as such throughout the library; the notation\n$a^{1/2}$ introduced here is the same number. What is new is the passage to\ngeneral $n$: the completed square that drives the $n = 2$ argument has no direct\nanalogue, and its place is taken by the factorisation of $b^n - a^n$ and the\nresulting Lipschitz estimate ([[lem-power-difference-factorisation]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$,\n$$\\frac{x^m}{\\exp(ax)}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(x\\to+\\infty).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each configuration, the product $\\prod_{i=1}^N T_{s_i,s_{i+1}}$ has bond contribution $e^{\\beta J\\sum_i s_is_{i+1}}$ and field contribution $e^{\\beta h\\sum_i(s_i+s_{i+1})/2}=e^{\\beta h\\sum_i s_i}$. These factors equal $e^{-\\beta H_N(s)}$. Summing over the finite spin sequences gives $Z_N=\\sum_{s_1,\\ldots,s_N}T_{s_1s_2}\\cdots T_{s_Ns_1}=\\operatorname{tr}(T^N)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In the ordered basis $+1,-1$, $T=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{\\beta J+\\beta h}&e^{-\\beta J}\\\\e^{-\\beta J}&e^{\\beta J-\\beta h}\\end{pmatrix}$. Put $A=e^{\\beta J}\\cosh(\\beta h)$ and $B=\\sqrt{e^{2\\beta J}\\sinh^2(\\beta h)+e^{-2\\beta J}}$. The diagonal entries give $\\operatorname{tr}T=2A$; direct multiplication gives $\\det T=e^{2\\beta J}-e^{-2\\beta J}$. Using $\\cosh^2x-\\sinh^2x=1$, which follows by expanding their definitions, $A^2-\\det T=e^{2\\beta J}\\sinh^2(\\beta h)+e^{-2\\beta J}=B^2$. Thus the characteristic polynomial has roots $A\\pm B=\\lambda_\\pm$. Since $A,B>0$, $A+B>|A-B|$, hence $\\lambda_+>|\\lambda_-|$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a $2\\times2$ matrix $M=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$, direct multiplication gives $M^2-(a+d)M+(ad-bc)I=0$. Apply this identity to $T$ and multiply by $T^n$; taking traces gives $z_{n+2}=(\\operatorname{tr}T)z_{n+1}-(\\det T)z_n$, where $z_n=\\operatorname{tr}(T^n)$, $z_0=2$, and $z_1=\\lambda_++\\lambda_-$. The sequence $\\lambda_+^n+\\lambda_-^n$ has the same recurrence and initial values, so induction gives $Z_N=z_N=\\lambda_+^N+\\lambda_-^N$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Set $r=\\lambda_-/\\lambda_+$. Then $Z_N=\\lambda_+^N(1+r^N)$ and $|r|<1$, so $1+r^N>0$. Therefore $N^{-1}\\log Z_N=\\log\\lambda_++N^{-1}\\log(1+r^N)$, whose second term tends to zero. For a compact parameter set, continuity and $|r|<1$ give $0<\\rho<1$ with $|r|\\le\\rho$. The positive radicand in $B$ is a smooth positive combination of exponentials; the real-power derivative in [F4] with exponent $1/2$, followed inductively by chain and product rules, makes $B$ and $\\lambda_\\pm$ smooth. Since $\\lambda_+>0$, its reciprocal and logarithm are smooth as well, by the real-power derivative with exponent $-1$ and the logarithm derivative in [F4]. Every fixed finite collection of derivatives of $r$ is therefore bounded on the compact set. For a multi-index $\\alpha$ of order at most $k$ and $N\\ge k$, repeated product differentiation of the $N$ factors gives at most $C_kN^k$ terms; each has at least $N-k$ undifferentiated factors $r$, so $|\\partial^\\alpha(r^N)|\\le C_kN^k\\rho^{N-k}$. Put $u=r^N$. Since $1+u\\ge1-\\rho$, repeated differentiation of $\\log(1+u)$ gives finite sums of products of derivatives of $u$ divided by powers of $1+u$. Each term has at least one derivative factor and the product bounds yield $|\\partial^\\alpha\\log(1+u)|\\le C'_kN^k\\rho^{N-k}$ after enlarging the constant. Divide by $N$; [F5] implies $N^{k-1}\\rho^{N-k}\\to0$. The zero-order logarithm term also tends uniformly to zero. Hence the correction and every fixed-order derivative vanish uniformly on the compact set.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement N>=2 fixes a nonempty spin ring with two-valued sites. Its finite spin sum has 2^N positive Boltzmann terms; the theorem asserts no zero-site pressure.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $\\beta>0$ and $J,h\\in\\mathbb R$. For $N\\ge2$, let $s_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, $s_{N+1}=s_1$, $H_N(s)=-J\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_is_{i+1}-h\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_i$, and $Z_N=\\sum_se^{-\\beta H_N(s)}$. Define $\\cosh x:=(e^x+e^{-x})/2$, $\\sinh x:=(e^x-e^{-x})/2$, and $T_{s,t}:=e^{\\beta Jst+\\beta h(s+t)/2}$. Then $Z_N=\\operatorname{tr}(T^N)=\\lambda_+^N+\\lambda_-^N$, where $\\lambda_\\pm=e^{\\beta J}\\cosh(\\beta h)\\pm\\sqrt{e^{2\\beta J}\\sinh^2(\\beta h)+e^{-2\\beta J}}$ and $\\lambda_+>|\\lambda_-|$. Therefore $N^{-1}\\log Z_N\\to\\log\\lambda_+$. On every compact subset of $(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^2$ in $(\\beta,J,h)$, the limit and its derivatives of every fixed finite order converge uniformly. This is a result for the specified one-dimensional periodic two-state model only.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "b08b603cbc41da359f55ccc1c426201bb221a913b4422457345fa0c1ff247e1b",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At J=h=0, T is the all-ones matrix, lambda+=2 and lambda-=0; steps 1.1–3.1 give Z_N=2^N."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For $N\\ge2$, let $s_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, $s_{N+1}=s_1$, $H_N(s)=-J\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_is_{i+1}-h\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_i$, and $Z_N=\\sum_se^{-\\beta H_N(s)}$. Boundary one: The claim is not a one-element counting or normalization assertion.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $\\beta>0$ and $J,h\\in\\mathbb R$. For $N\\ge2$, let $s_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, $s_{N+1}=s_1$, $H_N(s)=-J\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_is_{i+1}-h\\sum_{i=1}^Ns_i$, and $Z_N=\\sum_se^{-\\beta H_N(s)}$. Define $\\cosh x:=(e^x+e^{-x})/2$, $\\sinh x:=(e^x-e^{-x})/2$, and $T_{s,t}:=e^{\\beta Jst+\\beta h(s+t)/2}$. Then $Z_N=\\operatorname{tr}(T^N)=\\lambda_+^N+\\lambda_-^N$, where $\\lambda_\\pm=e^{\\beta J}\\cosh(\\beta h)\\pm\\sqrt{e^{2\\beta J}\\sinh^2(\\beta h)+e^{-2\\beta J}}$ and $\\lambda_+>|\\lambda_-|$. Therefore $N^{-1}\\log Z_N\\to\\log\\lambda_+$. On every compact subset of $(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^2$ in $(\\beta,J,h)$, the limit and its derivatives of every fixed finite order converge uniformly. This is a result for the specified one-dimensional periodic two-state model only.",
