        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-division-algorithm-in-z",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ ([[def-integers]]) with $b > 0$. Then there exist\nintegers $q$ and $r$ with\n\n$$a \\;=\\; qb + r, \\qquad 0 \\le r < b,$$\n\nand the pair $(q,r)$ with these two properties is unique.\n\nWe say $b$ **divides** $a$, written $b \\mid a$, when $a = qb$ for some\n$q \\in \\mathbb{Z}$; by the above, for $b > 0$ this holds exactly when the\nremainder $r$ is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $V=L^d$ and $J_0=\\sum_a\\|\\Phi_a\\|_\\infty$. Each configuration satisfies $|H_L(s)|\\le J_0V$, so $q^Ve^{-\\beta J_0V}\\le Z_L\\le q^Ve^{\\beta J_0V}$. Hence $p_L$ is bounded above and below uniformly in $L$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix an integer block length $\\ell>2R$ and write $L=k\\ell+r$ with $0\\le r<\\ell$ using [F3]. Partition $\\Lambda_L$ into $k^d$ disjoint $\\ell$-cubes and an outer remainder. Form $H_{L,\\ell}^{\\rm cut}$ by retaining only interactions whose entire support lies in one full cube and assigning zero energy to remainder sites. For a fixed shape, every eligible anchor lies in $[1-R,L+R]^d$. If its support crosses an internal block seam, its coordinate normal to that seam lies in at most $2R+1$ integer positions; the other coordinates have at most $(L+2R)^{d-1}$ choices. If it is not in such a seam strip but the interaction is omitted, its support meets the outer remainder, so its anchor lies within $\\ell+2R+1$ layers of one of the $d$ outer faces. Counting at most $d(k-1)$ internal seams and $d$ outer faces bounds the omitted anchors per shape by $d(2R+1)(L/\\ell)(L+2R)^{d-1}+d(\\ell+2R+1)(L+2R)^{d-1}$. Therefore $\\sup_s|H_L(s)-H_{L,\\ell}^{\\rm cut}(s)|\\le J_0d[(2R+1)(L/\\ell)+\\ell+2R+1](L+2R)^{d-1}$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If two energies on a finite state set differ pointwise by at most $D$, their partition sums satisfy $e^{-\\beta D}Z'\\le Z\\le e^{\\beta D}Z'$, so their log partition sums differ by at most $\\beta D$ using [F2]. The cut energy factorizes over the cubes and has zero-energy remainder, hence $Z_{L,\\ell}^{\\rm cut}=Z_\\ell^{k^d}q^{L^d-k^d\\ell^d}$. Divide the comparison by $L^d$ and let $L\\to\\infty$ with $\\ell$ fixed; the remainder fraction tends to zero, $k^d/L^d\\to\\ell^{-d}$, and the energy error has limsup at most $2^{d-1}\\beta J_0d(2R+1)/\\ell$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "It follows for every $\\ell>2R$ that $\\limsup_L p_L\\le p_\\ell+2^{d-1}\\beta J_0d(2R+1)/\\ell$ and $\\liminf_L p_L\\ge p_\\ell-2^{d-1}\\beta J_0d(2R+1)/\\ell$. Thus $0\\le\\limsup_Lp_L-\\liminf_Lp_L\\le2^d\\beta J_0d(2R+1)/\\ell$ for every such $\\ell$. Letting $\\ell\\to\\infty$ makes the difference zero; boundedness from step 1.1 then gives a finite limit.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $\\widetilde H_L$, the finite-state comparison of step 2.1 gives $|L^{-d}\\log\\widetilde Z_L-p_L|\\le\\beta C_b/L$, which tends to zero. Its limit is therefore the same.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
