    "thm-tdmath-limiting-derivative-and-concentration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-range-lattice-pressure-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every fixed $\\beta>0$, the sequence $p_L(\\beta)$ of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]] has a finite limit as $L\\to\\infty$. If another finite-volume energy $\\widetilde H_L$ on the same configurations satisfies $\\sup_s|\\widetilde H_L(s)-H_L(s)|\\le C_bL^{d-1}$ for a constant $C_b$ independent of $L$, define $\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta):=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal A^{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta\\widetilde H_L(s)}$ and $\\widetilde p_L(\\beta):=L^{-d}\\log\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta)$. Then $\\widetilde p_L$ has the same limit. The result covers finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range interactions on cubic boxes; it asserts no continuum-particle or long-range limit.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite probability space** is a pair $(\\Omega,w)$ consisting of a finite set $\\Omega$ and a function $w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $w(\\omega)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$ and\n$$\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)=1.$$\nThe elements of $\\Omega$ are **outcomes**. Every subset $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ is an **event**, and its probability is\n$$\\mathbb P(A):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega).$$\n\nAn outcome of weight zero remains an outcome. Thus an event can be nonempty and still have probability zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating the finite sum gives $p_L'=-L^{-d}\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}H_L$ and $p_L''=L^{-d}\\operatorname{Var}_{\\beta,L}(H_L)\\ge0$. Thus $p_L$ is convex. Passing the two-point convexity inequality to the pointwise limit shows that $p$ is convex too.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For any small $h>0$ with $\\beta\\pm h\\in J$, convexity and differentiability of $p_L$ give $[p_L(\\beta)-p_L(\\beta-h)]/h\\le p_L'(\\beta)\\le[p_L(\\beta+h)-p_L(\\beta)]/h$. Take lower and upper limits in $L$, use [F1], then let $h\\downarrow0$; differentiability of $p$ forces $p_L'(\\beta)\\to p'(\\beta)$. The identity in step 1.1 yields the claimed mean limit.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix $t>0$ small enough that $\\beta\\pm t\\in J$. For the upper tail, the pointwise inequality $\\mathbf1_{\\{H_L\\ge L^d(e+\\varepsilon)\\}}\\le e^{t(H_L-L^d(e+\\varepsilon))}$ and finite summation give $\\mathbb P(H_L/L^d\\ge e+\\varepsilon)\\le\\exp\\{L^d[p_L(\\beta-t)-p_L(\\beta)-t(e+\\varepsilon)]\\}$. Differentiability of $p$ and $e=-p'(\\beta)$ imply $p(\\beta-t)=p(\\beta)+te+o(t)$; choose $t$ so the bracket's limit is at most $-3t\\varepsilon/4$. Pointwise convergence at $\\beta-t$ and $\\beta$ then makes the bracket at most $-t\\varepsilon/2$ for all sufficiently large $L$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For the lower tail use $\\mathbf1_{\\{H_L\\le L^d(e-\\varepsilon)\\}}\\le e^{-t(H_L-L^d(e-\\varepsilon))}$; its expectation is $\\exp\\{L^d[p_L(\\beta+t)-p_L(\\beta)+t(e-\\varepsilon)]\\}$. Since $p(\\beta+t)=p(\\beta)-te+o(t)$, possibly reducing $t$ and increasing $L_0$ makes this at most $e^{-L^dt\\varepsilon/2}$. Adding both tail bounds yields the result with $c=t\\varepsilon/2$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Steps 1.2–2.2 bound the two tails whose union is $\\{|H_L/L^d-e|\\ge\\varepsilon\\}$; the theorem is conditional on differentiability of $p$ and does not assert convergence of second derivatives or equivalence for arbitrary observables.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.2",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
