      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-finite-range-lattice-pressure-limit",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-finite-range-lattice-pressure-limit.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every fixed $\\beta>0$, the sequence $p_L(\\beta)$ of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]] has a finite limit as $L\\to\\infty$. If another finite-volume energy $\\widetilde H_L$ on the same configurations satisfies $\\sup_s|\\widetilde H_L(s)-H_L(s)|\\le C_bL^{d-1}$ for a constant $C_b$ independent of $L$, define $\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta):=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal A^{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta\\widetilde H_L(s)}$ and $\\widetilde p_L(\\beta):=L^{-d}\\log\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta)$. Then $\\widetilde p_L$ has the same limit. The result covers finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range interactions on cubic boxes; it asserts no continuum-particle or long-range limit.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft result has a complete local proof carrier; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.2, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
          "carrier": "items/def-finite-probability-space-and-event.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **finite probability space** is a pair $(\\Omega,w)$ consisting of a finite set $\\Omega$ and a function $w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $w(\\omega)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$ and\n$$\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)=1.$$\nThe elements of $\\Omega$ are **outcomes**. Every subset $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ is an **event**, and its probability is\n$$\\mathbb P(A):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega).$$\n\nAn outcome of weight zero remains an outcome. Thus an event can be nonempty and still have probability zero.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "carrier": "items/def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "carrier": "items/def-variance-and-covariance.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "carrier": "items/def-real-exponential-function-and-e.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "carrier": "items/thm-derivative-of-exponential.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "carrier": "items/def-natural-logarithm.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "carrier": "items/thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/cor-mean-value-theorem.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F5.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 1.2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-tdmath-finite-ground-degeneracy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$,\n$$\\frac{x^m}{\\exp(ax)}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(x\\to+\\infty).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Factor $e^{-\\beta E_0}$ from the partition sum to obtain $Z=e^{-\\beta E_0}(g+\\sum_{i:E_i>E_0}e^{-\\beta(E_i-E_0)})$. Every gap is positive, and there are finitely many, so the parenthesized sum tends to $g$. Thus each ground probability tends to $1/g$ and every excited probability tends to zero.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Ground-state entropy terms tend to $-g(1/g)\\log(1/g)=\\log g$. For an excited state with gap $\\Delta_i>0$, step 1.1 gives $p_i=O(e^{-\\beta\\Delta_i})$ and $\\log p_i=-\\beta\\Delta_i-\\log D(\\beta)$, where $D(\\beta):=g+\\sum_{j:E_j>E_0}e^{-\\beta(E_j-E_0)}\\to g>0$ by step 1.1, so $\\log D(\\beta)$ is bounded by continuity of $\\log$; hence $|p_i\\log p_i|=O((1+\\beta)e^{-\\beta\\Delta_i})\\to0$ by [F3]. The excited sum is finite, so its limit is zero and the total entropy tends to $\\log g$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
