        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-integral-of-a-simple-function-against-a-pvm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space, let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space, let $E$ be a projection valued measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$,\nand let $s:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be a complex simple function\n([[def-complex-simple-function]], [[def-measurable-space]]). Present $s$ in\n**disjoint normal form with a zero-coefficient complement**, that is\n\n$$s=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_j\\mathbf 1_{B_j},$$\n\nwhere $m\\ge1$, and $B_1,\\dots,B_m\\in\\Sigma$ are pairwise disjoint with\n$B_1\\cup\\dots\\cup B_m=X$ and $a_1,\\dots,a_m\\in\\mathbb C$; a representation over\na disjoint family whose union misses some measurable set is completed by adding\nthat set with the coefficient $0$, and any coefficient is allowed to vanish.\nIn particular the zero function on the empty space uses $m=1$, $B_1=\\varnothing$\nand $a_1=0$; an empty presentation is not used.\nThen define\n\n$$\\int s\\,dE:=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_jE(B_j)\\in\\mathcal B(H).$$\n\nHere $\\mathbf 1_{B}$ is the indicator of $B$ and $E(B_j)$ is the value of the\nprojection valued measure on $B_j$ ([[def-projection-valued-measure]]).\n\n**Well-definedness.** Because the $B_j$ are pairwise disjoint and cover $X$,\nthe operator $\\sum_ja_jE(B_j)$ is a finite sum of bounded operators and hence a\nbounded operator; the sum is meaningful in $\\mathcal B(H)$ with the operator\nnorm, and $\\|\\sum_ja_jE(B_j)\\|\\le\\max_j|a_j|$, since the projection values are\ncontractive and pairwise orthogonal. The value displayed a priori depends on\nthe chosen disjoint presentation of $s$; that it does not,\n$\\int s\\,dE$ is independent of the disjoint presentation of $s$, is proved as\n[[lem-simple-pvm-integral-is-representation-independent]] immediately below,\nbefore the symbol is used. The normal-form convention $s=\\sum_ja_j\\mathbf 1_{B_j}$\nwith $B_1\\cup\\dots\\cup B_m=X$ is the one used throughout this page, and the\nidentity $\\int\\mathbf 1_B\\,dE=E(B)$ holds for every $B\\in\\Sigma$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-projection-valued-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space\n([[def-measurable-space]], [[def-sigma-algebra]]) and let $H$ be a complex\nHilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). A **projection valued measure** (PVM) on\n$(X,\\Sigma)$ is a map\n\n$$E:\\Sigma\\longrightarrow\\mathcal B(H),\\qquad B\\longmapsto E(B),$$\n\nsuch that:\n\n1. $E(B)$ is an **orthogonal projection** for every $B\\in\\Sigma$, that is, a\n   bounded operator with $E(B)^2=E(B)=E(B)^*$; there is no finite-dimensional\n   restriction;\n2. $E(\\varnothing)=0$ and $E(X)=I$;\n3. $E(B\\cap C)=E(B)E(C)$ for all $B,C\\in\\Sigma$;\n4. for every pairwise disjoint sequence $(B_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ in $\\Sigma$ with\n   union $B$ and every $x\\in H$, the series $\\sum_nE(B_n)x$ converges in norm\n   to $E(B)x$, that is\n   $$E(B)x=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}E(B_n)x .$$\n\nClause 4 is **strong countable additivity**. A PVM is called **regular** when\n$X$ is a locally compact Hausdorff space ([[def-locally-compact-space]]), the\n$\\sigma$-algebra is the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra, and every one of the finite\npositive measures\n\n$$E_x(B):=\\langle E(B)x,x\\rangle,\\qquad x\\in H,\\ B\\in\\Sigma,$$\n\nis a regular Borel measure ([[def-regular-borel-measure-on-an-lch-space]]). Inner\nproducts are linear in the first variable and conjugate-linear in the second\n([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]); this fixed convention is the one\nused for every pairing $E_{x,y}(B)=\\langle E(B)x,y\\rangle$ on this page.\n\n**Well-definedness: the projection clause.** The conditions $P^2=P=P^*$ are\nsimultaneously meaningful and describe exactly the Hilbert orthogonal\nprojections, with no hidden finite-dimensional hypothesis. Indeed, for a\nbounded $P$ with $P^2=P=P^*$ the range $\\operatorname{ran}P$ is a linear\nsubspace, and for $y=Pw$ one has\n$\\langle x-Px,y\\rangle=\\langle P(x-Px),w\\rangle=\\langle Px-P^2x,w\\rangle=0$, so\n$x-Px\\perp\\operatorname{ran}P$; conversely $\\langle Pz,Pz\\rangle=\\langle z,P^*Pz\\rangle=\\langle z,Pz\\rangle=0$\nfor $z\\perp\\operatorname{ran}P$, so $z\\in\\ker P$, while\n$\\langle z,Px\\rangle=\\langle Pz,x\\rangle=0$ for $z\\in\\ker P$ gives\n$\\ker P=(\\operatorname{ran}P)^\\perp$\n([[thm-hilbert-adjoint-properties]],\n[[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]). Hence\n$\\operatorname{ran}P=(\\ker P)^\\perp$ is closed, and the two defining properties\n$Px\\in\\operatorname{ran}P$, $x-Px\\in(\\operatorname{ran}P)^\\perp$ of\n[[def-hilbert-orthogonal-projection]] show that $P=P_{\\operatorname{ran}P}$ is\nthe Hilbert orthogonal projection onto a closed subspace, and conversely every\nsuch $P_M$ is idempotent and self-adjoint by\n[[lem-orthogonal-projection-is-linear-self-adjoint-contractive]] and\n[[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]]. Finally $P$ is\ncontractive: from $\\langle Px,x\\rangle=\\langle Px,Px\\rangle=\\|Px\\|^2$ and\nCauchy–Schwarz, $\\|Px\\|^2\\le\\|Px\\|\\,\\|x\\|$, so $\\|Px\\|\\le\\|x\\|$ for all $x$,\nand $\\langle Px,x\\rangle=\\|Px\\|^2\\ge0$ is a nonnegative real number.\n\n**Well-definedness: the regularity clause.** For an orthogonal projection value\nthe pairing $E_x(B)=\\langle E(B)x,x\\rangle$ is a nonnegative real number and\n$E_x(X)=\\langle x,x\\rangle=\\|x\\|^2<+\\infty$, so every $E_x$ is a finite\nnonnegative set function and the regularity requirement is a meaningful\ncondition on it; that each $E_x$ is genuinely a countably additive measure of\ntotal mass $\\|x\\|^2$, and that the polarized pairings $E_{x,y}$ are finite\ncomplex measures, is proved as\n[[lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm]] before either is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space, let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space, let $E$ be a projection valued measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$,\nand for $x,y\\in H$ define\n\n$$E_x(B):=\\langle E(B)x,x\\rangle,\\qquad E_{x,y}(B):=\\langle E(B)x,y\\rangle \\qquad(B\\in\\Sigma).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $E_x$ is a positive measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$ with $E_x(X)=\\|x\\|^2$ and\n   $0\\le E_x(B)\\le\\|x\\|^2$ for every $B$;\n2. $E_{x,y}$ is a finite complex measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$, the map\n   $(x,y)\\mapsto E_{x,y}$ is linear in $x$ and conjugate-linear in $y$, and\n   $E_{y,x}=\\overline{E_{x,y}}$, meaning $E_{y,x}(B)=\\overline{E_{x,y}(B)}$ for\n   every $B$;\n3. $|E_{x,y}|(X)\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$, so $|E_{x,y}(B)|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$ for every\n   $B$, and the polarization identity\n   $$E_{x,y}=\\frac14\\sum_{k=0}^{3}i^kE_{x+i^ky}$$\n   holds as an identity of complex measures, where $i^k$ are the fourth roots\n   of unity $1,i,-1,-i$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all vectors $x,y$ in a real or complex inner-product space,\n\n$$|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\| ,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed spaces over the same scalar field and let\n$T:X\\to Y$ be a bounded linear operator\n([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]) whose range $T(X)$ admits an ordered basis of\nfinite length ([[def-linear-basis]]). Then $T$ is compact\n([[def-compact-linear-operator]]).",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $X$ be a\nnormed space, let $Y$ be a Banach space ([[def-banach-space]]), and let\n$T_n:X\\to Y$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, be compact operators\n([[def-compact-linear-operator]]) with $\\|T_n-T\\|\\to0$ for a bounded linear\noperator $T:X\\to Y$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]],\n[[def-operator-norm]], [[def-metric-convergence]]). Then $T$ is compact.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f,g:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable and let $c\\ge0$.\n\n1. If $f\\le g$, then $\\int f\\,d\\mu\\le\\int g\\,d\\mu$.\n2. If $c>0$, then $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$. For $c=0$,\n   the integral of the zero function is $0$. Neither clause forms the\n   undefined extended-real product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $s$ is a nonnegative simple measurable function, then its nonnegative\nLebesgue integral equals its simple integral:\n$$\\int s\\,d\\mu=\\int_{\\mathrm{simple}} s\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\chi_{E_j}$ be a simple representation of a\nnonnegative simple measurable function\n([[def-nonnegative-simple-measurable-function]]) on a measure space\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$, so the $E_j\\in\\mathcal A$ are pairwise disjoint and\n$c_j\\ge0$. Its\n**simple integral** is\n$$\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mu(E_j),$$\nwhere $\\mu$ is the given measure ([[def-measure]]) and the convention\n$0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ is fixed once and for all.\n\nThe next lemma proves that this value is independent of the chosen simple\nrepresentation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\beta>0$, $-\\beta\\lambda\\le-\\beta a$ for $\\lambda\\ge a$; strict increase and positivity from [F11] show that $f_\\beta$ is bounded and strictly positive. Each branch is continuous and their values agree at $a$, so $f_\\beta$ is Borel. For each $n$, partition its bounded range into finitely many intervals of length at most $1/n$ and take their Borel preimages; choosing a nonnegative value in each interval gives a nonnegative real simple $s_n$ converging uniformly to $f_\\beta$. By [F13], $T_n:=\\int s_n\\,dE$ is a finite real linear combination of self-adjoint projections, hence self-adjoint and has nonnegative quadratic form. By [F2], $T_n\\to K_\\beta$ in operator norm. Therefore $\\langle K_\\beta x,y\\rangle=\\langle x,K_\\beta y\\rangle$ and $\\langle K_\\beta x,x\\rangle\\ge0$ by [F14] and passage to the limit, so $K_\\beta$ is self-adjoint and positive. If $K_\\beta x=0$, [F2] gives $\\int f_\\beta^2dE_x=0$. For each $n\\ge1$, $f_\\beta\\ge1/n$ on $C_n:=\\{\\lambda:f_\\beta(\\lambda)\\ge1/n\\}$; [F16] gives $0=\\int f_\\beta^2dE_x\\ge n^{-2}E_x(C_n)$, so $E_x(C_n)=0$. The sets $C_n$ increase to $\\mathbb R$; set $D_1=C_1$ and $D_n=C_n\\setminus C_{n-1}$ for $n>1$. They are disjoint, cover $\\mathbb R$, and satisfy $E_x(D_n)\\le E_x(C_n)=0$. Countable additivity in [F13] gives $E_x(\\mathbb R)=\\sum_nE_x(D_n)=0$, hence $\\|x\\|=0$ and $K_\\beta$ is injective.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F11",
            "F13",
            "F14",
            "F16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By the trace-class definition [F4], $K_\\beta$ is compact; together with step 1.1 it is compact self-adjoint and positive. Apply [F3]: its nonzero eigenspaces have finite multiplicity and span $(\\ker K_\\beta)^\\perp=\\mathcal H$, so AC supplies a finite or countable complete orthonormal family $(u_j)$ of eigenvectors with eigenvalues $\\kappa_j>0$. By [F4], $Z_\\beta=\\operatorname{Tr}K_\\beta=\\sum_j\\kappa_j<\\infty$; because $\\mathcal H\\ne0$ and $K_\\beta$ is injective, this sum is positive.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $j$. To justify the constant shift in the calculus, choose bounded simple $s_n\\to f_\\beta$ uniformly. By [F13], $\\int(s_n-\\kappa_j)\\,dE=\\int s_n\\,dE-\\kappa_jI$; [F2] passes both uniform limits, so $\\Phi_E(f_\\beta-\\kappa_j)=K_\\beta-\\kappa_jI$. Applying the norm identity in [F2] to this difference and the eigenvector gives $\\int|f_\\beta-\\kappa_j|^2dE_{u_j}=\\|(K_\\beta-\\kappa_jI)u_j\\|^2=0$. For every $n$, [F16] shows that the set where $|f_\\beta-\\kappa_j|\\ge1/n$ has zero $E_{u_j}$-measure; their union is the complement of the level set. Hence $E_{u_j}$ is supported on $f_\\beta^{-1}(\\kappa_j)\\cap[a,\\infty)=\\{E_j\\}$: injectivity of the exponential gives uniqueness, and [F12] gives $E_j=-\\beta^{-1}\\log\\kappa_j\\ge a$. Thus $\\int\\lambda^2dE_{u_j}=E_j^2<\\infty$ and [F1] yields $u_j\\in D(A)$ and $Au_j=E_ju_j$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "F16",
            "step 2.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The spectral measure of each $u_j$ is the unit mass at $E_j$ by step 3.1. Hence $\\|(I-E(\\{E_j\\}))u_j\\|^2=E_{u_j}(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{E_j\\})=0$, so $E(\\{E_j\\})u_j=u_j$; for Borel $D$ not containing $E_j$, $E(D)u_j=E(D)E(\\{E_j\\})u_j=E(\\varnothing)u_j=0$ by [F13]. For every $\\gamma>0$, choose nonnegative simple $s_n\\to f_\\gamma$ uniformly as in step 1.1. In each disjoint normal form, [F13] then gives $(\\int s_n\\,dE)u_j=s_n(E_j)u_j$; the uniform-limit clause of [F2] yields $K_\\gamma u_j=f_\\gamma(E_j)u_j=e^{-\\gamma E_j}u_j$. Fix $\\gamma\\in J$ and enumerate that basis; let $P_N$ project onto its first $N$ vectors, taking $P_N=I$ once a finite basis is exhausted. Parseval gives $\\|P_Nx\\|\\le\\|x\\|$, so $K_\\gamma P_N$ is bounded and finite rank. Fix the summable envelope $a_j$ for $\\{\\gamma\\}\\subset J$; every finite subsum of $t_j:=e^{-\\gamma E_j}$ is at most the corresponding sum of $a_j$, hence $S:=\\sum_jt_j<\\infty$ by the finite-subsum definition [F6]. This sum is positive because the basis is nonempty and every term is positive. For $\\varepsilon>0$, choose a finite set $F$ with $S-\\varepsilon<\\sum_{j\\in F}t_j$; once $N$ contains $F$, every finite tail subsum is at most $S-\\sum_{j\\in F}t_j<\\varepsilon$, so $\\sum_{j>N}t_j\\to0$. Again by Parseval, $$\\|(K_\\gamma-K_\\gamma P_N)x\\|^2=\\sum_{j>N}e^{-2\\gamma E_j}|\\langle x,u_j\\rangle|^2\\le\\Bigl(\\sum_{j>N}e^{-\\gamma E_j}\\Bigr)^2\\|x\\|^2,$$ so $K_\\gamma P_N$ converges to $K_\\gamma$ in operator norm. For $t_j=e^{-\\gamma E_j}$ its finite diagonal expansion is $K_\\gamma P_Nx=\\sum_{j\\le N}t_j\\langle x,u_j\\rangle u_j$; orthonormality and [F8] give $\\langle K_\\gamma P_Nx,y\\rangle=\\langle x,K_\\gamma P_Ny\\rangle$ and $\\langle K_\\gamma P_Nx,x\\rangle=\\sum_{j\\le N}t_j|\\langle x,u_j\\rangle|^2\\ge0$. Thus each truncation is self-adjoint and positive, and [F14] plus operator-norm convergence preserves both properties. It is compact by [F15], since the truncations are bounded finite-rank operators and $\\mathcal H$ is Banach. Thus $K_\\gamma$ is compact self-adjoint and positive. Its absolute value is $K_\\gamma$, by the unique positive square-root definition in [F9]. If $K_\\gamma x=\\lambda x$, taking coordinates in the complete basis gives $(t_j-\\lambda)\\langle x,u_j\\rangle=0$ for every $j$; thus its eigenvalues and their multiplicities are exactly the diagonal values $t_j$. Hence [F9] identifies its positive singular values, with multiplicity, as the list $(t_j)$. Their finite subsums are bounded by $\\sum_j a_j$, so the trace-class definition [F4] applies and gives $\\operatorname{Tr}K_\\gamma=\\sum_j t_j$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F13",
            "F14",
            "F15",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "3.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "At the fixed $\\beta$, $\\rho_\\beta=K_\\beta/Z_\\beta$ is positive trace class and [F5] gives $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_\\beta=Z_\\beta^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}K_\\beta=1$. For each $\\gamma\\in J$, $\\rho_\\gamma=K_\\gamma/Z_\\gamma$ likewise has trace one; its eigenvalues $p_j=e^{-\\gamma E_j}/Z_\\gamma$ form the canonical law, so [F6] gives the finite mean, variance, and identity $-\\sum_jp_j\\log p_j=\\log Z_\\gamma+\\gamma\\sum_jp_jE_j$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "For bounded $B$, [F5] makes $\\rho_\\beta B$ and $B\\rho_\\beta$ trace class and [F7] gives $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_\\beta B)=\\operatorname{Tr}(B\\rho_\\beta)$. Evaluate the second trace in the complete basis $(u_j)$: $\\operatorname{Tr}(B\\rho_\\beta)=\\sum_j\\langle B\\rho_\\beta u_j,u_j\\rangle=\\sum_jp_j\\langle Bu_j,u_j\\rangle$, where [F8] and real $p_j$ justify pulling the scalar out. This series is absolutely convergent since $|\\langle Bu_j,u_j\\rangle|\\le\\|B\\|$ and $\\sum_jp_j=1$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
