            "F2",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Divide the first derivative by $Z>0$ and use [F4] to obtain $\\partial_a\\log Z=\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a$. The quotient rule for the normalized first moment gives $\\partial_b\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a=\\mathbb E_\\theta(X_aX_b)-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a\\mathbb E_\\theta X_b$, the covariance.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "For any $v\\in\\mathbb R^r$, $v^T(\\nabla^2\\log Z)v=\\mathbb E_\\theta[(v\\cdot X-\\mathbb E_\\theta(v\\cdot X))^2]\\ge0$. This proves positive semidefiniteness without any independence assumption.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement 0<Z(theta)<infinity excludes a null weighted base measure or an empty effective support. The observable list and open parameter domain remain those explicitly fixed; no normalized law is assigned when Z=0.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $w:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $X_a:X\\to\\mathbb R$ ($1\\le a\\le r$) be measurable, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ be open. For $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_r)$, define $\\theta\\cdot X(x):=\\sum_{a=1}^r\\theta_aX_a(x)$. Set $Z(\\theta):=\\int w\\exp(\\theta\\cdot X)\\,d\\mu$ and assume $0<Z(\\theta)<\\infty$ on $U$. Suppose for every compact $K\\subset U$ there is an integrable $G_K\\ge0$ such that $w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}(1+\\sum_a|X_a|^2)\\le G_K$ pointwise for all $\\theta\\in K$. For the normalized density $p_\\theta=w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}/Z(\\theta)$, define $\\mathbb E_\\theta Y:=\\int Yp_\\theta\\,d\\mu$ when integrable and $\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b):=\\mathbb E_\\theta[(X_a-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a)(X_b-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_b)]$. Then $\\partial_a\\log Z=\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a$ and $\\partial_b\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b)$. Thus the Hessian of $\\log Z$ is positive semidefinite. This is a mathematical model identity; it assumes no independence or absence of correlations.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "90ccb2f6a2cc883c165e69ae7be5db39194c6b3b87a2ee423eefc7780642ed55",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "If all observables vanish, the derivatives and covariance matrix vanish; step 3.1 gives a zero quadratic form."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $w:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $X_a:X\\to\\mathbb R$ ($1\\le a\\le r$) be measurable, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ be open. For $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_r)$, define $\\theta\\cdot X(x):=\\sum_{a=1}^r\\theta_aX_a(x)$. Set $Z(\\theta):=\\int w\\exp(\\theta\\cdot X)\\,d\\mu$ and assume $0<Z(\\theta)<\\infty$ on $U$. Suppose for every compact $K\\subset U$ there is an integrable $G_K\\ge0$ such that $w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}(1+\\sum_a|X_a|^2)\\le G_K$ pointwise for all $\\theta\\in K$. For the normalized density $p_\\theta=w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}/Z(\\theta)$, define $\\mathbb E_\\theta Y:=\\int Yp_\\theta\\,d\\mu$ when integrable and $\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b):=\\mathbb E_\\theta[(X_a-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a)(X_b-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_b)]$. Then $\\partial_a\\log Z=\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a$ and $\\partial_b\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b)$. Thus the Hessian of $\\log Z$ is positive semidefinite. This is a mathematical model identity; it assumes no independence or absence of correlations.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "90ccb2f6a2cc883c165e69ae7be5db39194c6b3b87a2ee423eefc7780642ed55",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "evidence": "Statement and proof 2.1 admit a singleton index or a single supplied state/particle in the given measurable weighted exponential family with positive finite partition and its finite-dimensional covariance Hessian. The coordinate identity, positive normalization or cell estimate is the same stated formula; no separate multi-index lower bound is required."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $w:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $X_a:X\\to\\mathbb R$ ($1\\le a\\le r$) be measurable, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ be open. For $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_r)$, define $\\theta\\cdot X(x):=\\sum_{a=1}^r\\theta_aX_a(x)$. Set $Z(\\theta):=\\int w\\exp(\\theta\\cdot X)\\,d\\mu$ and assume $0<Z(\\theta)<\\infty$ on $U$. Suppose for every compact $K\\subset U$ there is an integrable $G_K\\ge0$ such that $w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}(1+\\sum_a|X_a|^2)\\le G_K$ pointwise for all $\\theta\\in K$. For the normalized density $p_\\theta=w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}/Z(\\theta)$, define $\\mathbb E_\\theta Y:=\\int Yp_\\theta\\,d\\mu$ when integrable and $\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b):=\\mathbb E_\\theta[(X_a-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a)(X_b-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_b)]$. Then $\\partial_a\\log Z=\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a$ and $\\partial_b\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b)$. Thus the Hessian of $\\log Z$ is positive semidefinite. This is a mathematical model identity; it assumes no independence or absence of correlations.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "90ccb2f6a2cc883c165e69ae7be5db39194c6b3b87a2ee423eefc7780642ed55",
