            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $w:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $X_a:X\\to\\mathbb R$ ($1\\le a\\le r$) be measurable, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ be open. For $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_r)$, define $\\theta\\cdot X(x):=\\sum_{a=1}^r\\theta_aX_a(x)$. Set $Z(\\theta):=\\int w\\exp(\\theta\\cdot X)\\,d\\mu$ and assume $0<Z(\\theta)<\\infty$ on $U$. Suppose for every compact $K\\subset U$ there is an integrable $G_K\\ge0$ such that $w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}(1+\\sum_a|X_a|^2)\\le G_K$ pointwise for all $\\theta\\in K$. For the normalized density $p_\\theta=w e^{\\theta\\cdot X}/Z(\\theta)$, define $\\mathbb E_\\theta Y:=\\int Yp_\\theta\\,d\\mu$ when integrable and $\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b):=\\mathbb E_\\theta[(X_a-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a)(X_b-\\mathbb E_\\theta X_b)]$. Then $\\partial_a\\log Z=\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a$ and $\\partial_b\\mathbb E_\\theta X_a=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X_a,X_b)$. Thus the Hessian of $\\log Z$ is positive semidefinite. This is a mathematical model identity; it assumes no independence or absence of correlations.",
