      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-measure-integral.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where the Borel sets of the extended real line are the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft definition is present; its local interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f_n:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable with $f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere. Suppose $g\\ge0$ is measurable and $\\int g\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for every $n$. Then $f$ is integrable, $\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\to\\int f\\,d\\mu$, and $\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\to0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft result has a complete local proof carrier; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "carrier": "items/def-real-exponential-function-and-e.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "carrier": "items/thm-derivative-of-exponential.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "carrier": "items/def-natural-logarithm.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "carrier": "items/thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/cor-mean-value-theorem.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F5.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-chain-rule",
          "carrier": "items/thm-chain-rule.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F6.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "carrier": "items/thm-algebra-of-derivatives.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F6.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        }
      ]
