    "lem-tdmath-bose-fermi-mode-fluctuations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I,E_i,g_i,J,Z$ be as in [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]]. Suppose that for every compact interval $K\\subset J$ there is a summable sequence $a_i\\ge0$ such that $g_i(1+|E_i|^2)\\exp(-\\beta E_i)\\le a_i$ for every $i\\in I$ and $\\beta\\in K$. Then $Z$ is positive, finite, and $C^2$ on $J$, with $Z'(\\beta)=-\\sum_i g_iE_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ and $Z''(\\beta)=\\sum_i g_iE_i^2\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$. The probabilities $p_i$ are normalized; their mean $\\bar E$ and variance are finite and satisfy $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E$ and $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$. Moreover $H_g(p)=\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E$, and its defining series is absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite probability space** is a pair $(\\Omega,w)$ consisting of a finite set $\\Omega$ and a function $w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $w(\\omega)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$ and\n$$\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)=1.$$\nThe elements of $\\Omega$ are **outcomes**. Every subset $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ is an **event**, and its probability is\n$$\\mathbb P(A):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega).$$\n\nAn outcome of weight zero remains an outcome. Thus an event can be nonempty and still have probability zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$,\n$$\\frac{x^m}{\\exp(ax)}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(x\\to+\\infty).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $S_N=\\sum_{n=0}^Nq^n$. Multiplying this finite sum by $1-q$ gives $(1-q)S_N=1-q^{N+1}$. Since $q=e^{-\\gamma}$ for $\\gamma>0$, [F5] gives $q^{N+1}\\to0$; hence $Z=(1-q)^{-1}$ and the weights $(1-q)q^n$ sum to one. On a compact $\\gamma$-interval $[b,B]\\subset(0,\\infty)$, set $a_n=(1+n^2)e^{-bn}$. Then $a_{n+1}/a_n=e^{-b}(1+(n+1)^2)/(1+n^2)\\to e^{-b}<1$. Choose $c$ with $e^{-b}<c<1$; for all sufficiently large $n$, $a_{n+1}\\le ca_n$, so the tail is bounded by a convergent geometric series. Thus $\\sum_n(1+n^2)e^{-\\gamma n}\\le\\sum_na_n<\\infty$, which is the local envelope required by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "With $Z(\\gamma)=(1-e^{-\\gamma})^{-1}$, [F1] gives $\\mathbb E n=-\\partial_\\gamma\\log Z=q/(1-q)$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(n)=\\partial_\\gamma^2\\log Z=q/(1-q)^2$. Substitution $q=e^{-\\gamma}$ and [F4] justify the parameter derivatives. Therefore $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(n)}/\\mathbb E n=1/\\sqrt q\\to1$ as $q\\uparrow1$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The fermionic partition sum is $1+q$; its two probabilities are $1/(1+q)$ and $q/(1+q)$. Their finite weighted sums give mean $q/(1+q)$ and variance $q/(1+q)^2$, equal to the mean times one minus the mean.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "These are calculations for the two specified probability laws only; no independence, physical preparation, or measured fluctuation claim is used.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "2.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
