          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "carrier": "items/thm-natural-logarithm-laws.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-chain-rule",
          "carrier": "items/thm-chain-rule.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F4.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "carrier": "items/thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial.md",
          "status": "current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$,\n$$\\frac{x^m}{\\exp(ax)}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(x\\to+\\infty).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F5.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical result; proof carrier is present and its hypotheses were inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-tdmath-countable-partition-divergence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Every summand is $g_ne^{-\\beta E_n}=1$, so the first $N$ terms sum to $N$ for every $N$; their supremum is infinite.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Thus $Z(\\beta)=+\\infty$, which violates the finite-normalization condition in [F1]; no normalized canonical probabilities $p_n$ are defined.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
