          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F1.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft definition is present; its local interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier carrier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $I,E_i,g_i,J,Z$ be as in [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]]. Suppose that for every compact interval $K\\subset J$ there is a summable sequence $a_i\\ge0$ such that $g_i(1+|E_i|^2)\\exp(-\\beta E_i)\\le a_i$ for every $i\\in I$ and $\\beta\\in K$. Then $Z$ is positive, finite, and $C^2$ on $J$, with $Z'(\\beta)=-\\sum_i g_iE_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ and $Z''(\\beta)=\\sum_i g_iE_i^2\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$. The probabilities $p_i$ are normalized; their mean $\\bar E$ and variance are finite and satisfy $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E$ and $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$. Moreover $H_g(p)=\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E$, and its defining series is absolutely convergent.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact current supplier statement is quoted above. Its hypotheses, domain and conventions are those declared by consumer Fact labels F2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft result has a complete local proof carrier; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-tdmath-correlated-pair-mutual-information": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite random variables $M,R$ with joint law $p(m,r)$ and marginals $p_M,p_R$, use the entropy definition $H(Y)$ of [[lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy]] for $Y=M,R,(M,R)$. Define $I(M;R):=\\sum_{p(m,r)>0}p(m,r)\\log\\frac{p(m,r)}{p_M(m)p_R(r)}$. Then $I(M;R)=H(M)+H(R)-H(M,R)=H(M)-H(M|R)\\ge0$, with equality exactly when $p(m,r)=p_M(m)p_R(r)$ for every pair.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-conditional-memory-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ and $R$ take values in finite sets under a joint probability law $p(m,r)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{m,r}p(m,r)=1$. For any finite random variable $Y$ with law $p_Y$, define its Shannon entropy by $H(Y):=-\\sum_{y:p_Y(y)>0}p_Y(y)\\log p_Y(y)$; for a pair, $H(M,R)$ means this definition applied to its joint law. Put $a_r:=\\sum_m p(m,r)$ and, when $a_r>0$, $q(m|r):=p(m,r)/a_r$. Thus $H(R)=-\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r\\log a_r$. Define $H(M|R):=\\sum_{r:a_r>0}a_r[-\\sum_{m:q(m|r)>0}q(m|r)\\log q(m|r)]$. Then $H(M,R)=H(R)+H(M|R)$ and $H(M|R)\\ge0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Both marginals are uniform, so their entropies are $-2(1/2)\\log(1/2)=\\log2$. The joint law has two positive entries of size $1/2$, so $H(M,R)=\\log2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Conditional on either value of $R$, $M$ is determined, so its conditional law has one probability $1$ and one $0$; therefore $H(M|R)=0$. Substituting these values into [F1] gives $I(M;R)=\\log2$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
