            "source_excerpt": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "20e1534f315a2ea5e3f7779668b844ea41d9fc69faa1552d140c2df894b3c65d",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
